uld be and they literally  said to me that sounds great are you in  and I was like whoa you saying I can  make that movie and they said yeah I  said okay what's the caveat and they  said the caveat is these movies take  like three years but you're going to  have to and I didn't know what that  meant because I was stupid I had no idea  I hadn't done you know any any and so  that's how I got into that and then on  this one they liked the movie so much on  this one at the end they said hey listen  why don't you do another one and I said  great I would love to and you know we  knew we wanted it to be this kind of  last leg of the Caesar journey  but there was no story that we hadn't  talked about it being trilogy and they  said listen you did that one in two  years how about this one  and I was like No so this one took three  years Joey you've been involved in all  three of the films how has the  technology changed in the amount of  years from the first one to the third  one  have there been leaps and bounds well  the technology change really happened  the big change was for the first film we  we'd been lucky to work with Andy Serkis  a lot in the past we done Gollum and  King Kong together he played you know  Captain Haddock when we did Tintin so  we've been using this idea of  performance captured to render Andy's  performance to the screen for quite a  long time but it was always a process  where Andy would be there for the actors  like well you know like say when he was  doing Gollum Peter would be shooting and  he was in there the actors were working  with them they benefited from that give  and take but Andy then had to go back  and redo this performance for us in what  was basically a gym with a bunch of  cameras around it was very much like a  science experiment and you know we  thought when it came time to do then  once we knew Andy was was in a Caesar he  was going to do the part I thought we  really owe it to him to get that  performance he's giving on the day you  know I don't want to say to Andy after  all this time can you do it again so we  really set to mind how can we capture it  live and there's you know you're dealing  with with cameras and lights and they  tend to interfere with the film cameras  and light so that's that's been the  basic problem but we figured out a way  to crack that and we would hang our  cameras around the set and it became  part of the live action shooting so in  the first film which was mostly you know  on stages but also some exterior  locations we had Andy working with the  actors and the performance that he gave  was the performance that made it to the  film so for us that that was the big  breakthrough after that Matt decided to  make it hard decided he decided that  okay that's great that that works but it  really allows us to take the story  elsewhere you know to take it to the ape  village and then to take it on this  great Exodus and you know to me I think  about it in the way that like it must  have been in the days when you you we're  doing sound you had a big blimp on the  camera  couldn't move it anywhere then someday  you figured out how to make it lighter  and move it a little bit farther and  that's kind of where I think we're going  with it you know like if Matt says I  wanted you to do the next one  wherever it might be we want to be ready  for that because that's really what this  is all about it's just it's being there  to help tell the story  huge Steve Zahn fan I've loved him from  that thing you do in suburbia  - happy Texas that all the other great  stuff he's done how did you know in  meeting with him that he could do this  very particular kind of acting job that  you need to be able to do for this okay  so that's the perfect question he wasn't  doing a certain kind of acting job in  the sense that I think people think you  have to understand that this the  performance capture is exactly that it's  a system of cameras that records a  performance you've got tracking markers  you've got cameras and what are the  actors doing they're performing and  that's it there's no like learning like  everyone talks about like you know Andy  Serkis he's the greatest mocap actor in  history which is literally a meaningless  designation because what he is is one of  the greatest actors on the planet and  he's so incredible and and you have to  understand that the artistry that it  takes in this movie to get that  performance comes from a couple places  one is first with his performance he's  incredible and then the other thing that  happens is these incredible artists at  whether they are translating that  performance and the reason has to be  translated is because you have Andy's  face and when you look at Andy's face he  makes you feel a certain way and then  you want Caesar to make you feel the  same way but it's a different Anatomy so  that requires that's not like hey you  know some people think oh well Andy just  is the voice no Andy is the performance  and some people think oh well whether  they have all these technical things and  they push a button know what they are is  all these incredible animators who have  to find out the way what we do is we  take the shots of the actors and we put  them up side by side with every  submission of the performance that's  that's been animated and we look at it  and go oh you know Andy looks he looks  really angry but he's also sad and it's  that mix of emotions that makes you feel  that and Caeser right now I can see the  anger but the sadness is not there yet  and then we get into this discussion  with the enemies oh yeah well that's  because Andy on his brow he's got this  shape but Caesar doesn't have that and I  go we need that don't we nee go well we  have to figure out how to get that shape  to be there because we experience  emotion based on the expressions we see  on actors faces so this is the crazy  alchemy that comes together is  translating shot by shot all of those  things so that was